'''''Cheungkongella''''' is a fossil from the [[lower Cambrian]] [[Chengjiang lagerstatte]], the affinity of which has been the subject of debate.  It was announced as the oldest known [[tunicate]], although this affinity was disputed in later paper announcing the discovery of ''[[Shankouclava]]'', also from Chengjiang, as the oldest known tunicate.<ref name=Gee>{{harvnb|Gee|2018|page=99}}</ref>  A third Cambrian tunicate, ''[[Megasiphon]]'', is from the somewhat younger [[Marjum Formation]] of Utah.


'''''Shankouclava''''' is an extinct genus of [[tunicate]]s. It represents the oldest candidate member of this group, dating to {{Ma|Chengjiang}}. It has been found in the [[Lower Cambrian]] [[Maotianshan Shale]] at Shankou village, [[Anning, Yunnan|Anning]], near [[Kunming]] ([[South China]]). Each of the eight specimens found and used for description were isolated, suggesting that the genus was [[Solitary animal|solitary]] and not [[Colony (biology)|colonial]].<ref name=Chen2003>{{harvnb|Chen|Huang|Peng|Chi|2003}}</ref>

==Classification==

===Debate over relationship to ''Phlogites''===
[[File:Cheungkongella and Phlogites.png|thumb|left|Cheungkongella ancestralis (left) and Phlogites (right) fossils for comparison<br />{{small|(BT: branching tentacles; CIS: Complex internal organs; Os: Oral siphon; S: Stem; Sp: Simple pharynx; St: Stomach}})]]

==Morphology==
''Shankouclava'' had a soft, sac-like body that was elongated and pointed proximally. The body lengths of individuals vary from 2&nbsp;cm (0.8&nbsp;in) to 4&nbsp;cm (1.6&nbsp;in).<ref name=Chen2003 />

Degan Shu asserts that some fossil specimens exhibit tentacles, which would make the genus similar to the [[deuterostome]] ''[[Phlogites]]''.<ref>{{harvnb|Shu|2008}}</ref> Expanding on this idea, Shu and colleagues argue against a [[tunicate]] affinity for ''Shankouclava''.<ref>{{harvnb|Shu|Conway Morris|Zhang|Han|2010}}</ref>

==Etymology==
The generic name is composed of the fossil locality, Shankou, and the [[Latin]] word ''clava'' (club-shaped).<ref name=Chen2003 />

==XClassification==
A stem group tunicate affinity for ''Shankouclava'' has been broadly accepted, though some authors have expressed reservations that the evidence in favour of this affinity is not conclusive.<ref>{{harvnb|Conway Morris|2006}}</ref>

Another Cambrian tunicate, ''[[Megasiphon]]'', known from a single specimen, resembles more closely the morphology of surviving tunicates.<ref>{{harvnb|Nanglu|Lerosey-Aubril|Weaver|Ortega-Hernández|2023}}</ref>

